![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The current implementation of `_build_message_id_index` performs redundant calculations by calling `.get("message_id", "").strip()` twice for every email in the list (once for the key and once for the condition). For large batches of emails, this can be optimized by using a standard loop.

```suggestion
def _build_message_id_index(emails) -> dict:
    """Build a lookup map from message_id to email dict."""
    index = {}
    for email in emails:
        msg_id = email.get("message_id", "").strip()
        if msg_id:
            index[msg_id] = email
    return index
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

While the `for-else` pattern reduces the number of return statements, this logic can be made even more concise and arguably more readable by using the built-in `any()` function with a generator expression. This approach encapsulates the matching logic in a helper function and simplifies the control flow.

```python
    # Check against allowlist
    def is_match(allowed_path: str) -> bool:
        if allowed_path.endswith("/"):
            # Prefix match (subtree): rel_path == "todo" or starts with "todo/"
            prefix = allowed_path.rstrip("/")
            return rel_path == prefix or rel_path.startswith(allowed_path)
        # Exact match
        return rel_path == allowed_path

    return any(is_match(allowed) for allowed in MAIN_BRANCH_ALLOWLIST)
```

